rsday 8 Oct 2026.\n\nLet me run the targeted call.","details":[{"type":"text","text":"The review: Thu 8 Oct 2026, 15:00 UTC. Ask: what else is on my calendar that day (8 Oct 2026, 00:00–23:59 UTC across all calendars, all visible calendars for ana@demo.trymetis.email).\n\nFrom the earlier full-window list, 8 Oct events I saw:\n- Edge cases: \"Daily forever\" 09:00–09:15 (rrule)\n- Work: \"Daily standup\" 09:00–09:15\n- Work: \"Workshop: packaging prototypes\" 10:00–13:00, Studio 2, attendees ana, raj, priya\n- Work: \"Print vendor call (Pune)\" 13:00–13:30, priya\n- Work: \"Design review\" 15:00–16:00, Studio 2\n- That''s it in the 250-cap list for Oct 8? The list was truncated at Nov 13 but included all of Sep 1–Oct 8 fully? The 250 cap: entries were sorted by start time, so Sep 1 through ~Nov 13 included. It returned exactly 250 entries and the last was Nov 13 12:00. So Oct 8 entries are all included (before the cap). But there might be Bulk calendar entries for Oct 8? Bulk entries I saw started at Nov 1 in the output... Actually bulk-0 through bulk-292 — I saw them starting Nov 1. Hmm, bulk entries appeared only from Nov 1 onwards, suggesting Bulk calendar has entries only in November. But there could be bulk entries in October that I didn''t see... no, the list is sorted by start, and Oct 8 events would appear before Nov 1. The truncated list ended at Nov 13 12:45. So Oct 8 range is complete within the returned data. Wait — is it sorted ascending? Yes, \"merged and sorted by start time\". The list began Sep 1, ... progressed through Oct, then Bulk entries from Nov 1, ending Nov 13. So yes, complete for Oct 8.\n\nBut to be safe and accurate, I should just do a targeted query for Oct 8 only: timeMin 2026-10-08T00:00:00Z, timeMax 2026-10-09T00:00:00Z, max 250. That''s cleaner. Let me do that.\n\nNote the day is Thursday 8 Oct 2026.\n\nLet me run the targeted call."}],"createdAt":1794305982679},{"type":"tool-invocation","toolInvocation":{"state":"result","result":{"