 12 2026 (read)\n- token 311: 50 items Apr 11 → Jan 12 2026 (read)\n- token 301: 50 items Jan 12 2026 → Nov 2024? (the batch that returned Jan 1 2026 ... Jun 2025?) Hmm.\n- token 261: 10 items (edge att/jsonld, unread)\n- token 251: 50 items (edge long thread, unread) → token 201\n\nSo there are more pages. The remaining: token 201 .. down to 0. The thread is 200 messages (edge-thread-200), ids 201-250 are index positions... Actually ids like \"WyJJTkJPWCIsIjEwMDAiLDIwMV0\" are base64 of [\"INBOX\",\"1000\",201]. So the INBOX index goes up to at least 410 (e.g. index 410 = Kestrel Sep 26). Wait, index 410 at newest? The newest inbox item is index 410. So INBOX holds indexes up to ~410, and pages descend 410 → 1. So the inbox has roughly 410 emails total(!), of which many are edge fixtures.\n\nHmm wait: index 401-410 are Sep 2026 items; 261-300 edge/jsonld; 201-250 edge long thread; presumably 1-200 more stuff.\n\nSo counting unread requires walking ~410 items = 9 pages. I''ve seen several. Feasible but heavy: remaining pages: token 201, and whatever follows. Let me just page through with limit 50 repeatedly until nextPageToken is absent. That''s maybe 7 more calls. Do them in parallel batches where possible (tokens are sequential, but I can guess: after 201 comes 151? The tokens seem to be the next index start: 201 means next page starts at index 201 → after fetching, next token 151. Actually pattern: token values observed: 401, 361, 351, 311, 301, 261, 251, 201. Differences are 50 sometimes (401→351? no I got 351 from page 401 and also called 361 which returned items Aug 4 → Apr 12).\n\nConfusing: 361 call returned Aug 4→Apr 12 which overlaps with 351 (Jul 22→Jan 12). Hmm, so tokens aren''t strictly index-based.\n\nSafer: sequential paging one at a time. But that''s many turns. Given the tool guidance to batch independent calls, I can try guessing sequential tokens in one batch: 201, 151, 101, 51, 1 — if each page returns 50 with next token 151, 101, 51, 1, then done. Risk: if a wrong token yields an error or duplicates, I''d notice. Let''s try batch: 201, 151, 101, 51, 1. If some fail, retry.\n\nWait — do I even need full precision? The user asks how many unread in the inbox right now. A precise number is expected. Let''s enumerate.\n\nLet me batch: pageToken 201, 151, 101, 51, 1. I''ll count unread per page myself.\n\nActually another thought: maybe most of the \"edge\" fixtures (att-*, jsonld-*, html-*) are unread and there are ~100 of them. Plus the 200-message long thread all unread. So unread could be ~280 of ~410. Let''s just count.\n\nProceed with the batch of 5 pages.","details":[{"type":"text","text":"The inbox contains a long \"Re: Long thread\" edge thread (edge test fixtures) — ids 201..250 shown, all unread. This is a huge thread of 200 messages (edge-thread-200). Plus other edge mails.\n\nCounting unread precisely requires paging through the whole inbox. The inbox seems to have at least 310+ items. Let''s estimate: this is genuinely large. Enumeration: pages seen so far:\n- page1 (0): 50 items, latest Sep 26 → Sep 18 (all read)\n- token 401: 50 items Sep 18 → Jul 22 (all read)\n- token 361: 50 items Aug 4 → Apr 12 (all read)\n- token 351: 50 items Jul 22 → Jan 12 2026 (read)\n- token 311: 50 items Apr 11 → Jan 12 2026 (read)\n- token 301: 50 items Jan 12 2026 → Nov 2024? (the batch that returned Jan 1 2026 ... Jun 2025?) Hmm.\n- token 261: 10 items (edge att/jsonld, unread)\n- token 251: 50 items (edge long thread, unread) → token 201\n\nSo there are more pages. The remaining: token 201 .. down to 0. The thread is 200 messages (edge-thread-200), ids 201-250 are index positions... Actually ids like \"WyJJTkJPWCIsIjEwMDAiLDIwMV0\" are base64 of [\"INBOX\",\"1000\",201]. So the INBOX index goes up to at least 410 (e.g. index 410 = Kestrel Sep 26). Wait, index 410 at newest? The newest inbox item is index 410. So INBOX holds indexes up to ~410, and pages descend 410 → 1. So the inbox has roughly 410 emails total(!), of which many are edge fixtures.\n\nHmm wait: index 401-410 are Sep 2026 items; 261-300 edge/jsonld; 201-250 edge long thread; presumably 1-200 more stuff.\n\nSo counting unread requires walking ~410 items = 9 pages. I''ve seen several. Feasible but heavy: remaining pages: token 201, and whatever follows. Let me just page through with limit 50 repeatedly until nextPageToken is absent. That''s maybe 7 more calls. Do them in parallel batches where possible (tokens are sequential, but I can guess: after 201 comes 151? The tokens seem to be the next index start: 201 means next page starts at index 201 → after fetching, next token 151. Actually pattern: token values observed: 401, 361, 351, 311, 301, 261, 251, 201. Differences are 50 sometimes (401→351? no I got 351 from page 401 and also called 361 which returned items Aug 4 → Apr 12).\n\nConfusing: 361 call returned Aug 4→Apr 12 which overlaps with 351 (Jul 22→Jan 12). Hmm, so tokens aren''t strictly index-based.\n\nSafer: sequential paging one at a time. But that''s many turns. Given the tool guidance to batch independent calls, I can try guessing sequential tokens in one batch: 201, 151, 101, 51, 1 — if each page returns 50 with next token 151, 101, 51, 1, then done. Risk: if a wrong token yields an error or duplicates, I''d notice. Let''s try batch: 201, 151, 101, 51, 1. If some fail, retry.\n\nWait — do I even need full precision? The user asks how many unread in the inbox right now. A precise number is expected. Let''s enumerate.\n\nLet me batch: pageToken 201, 151, 101, 51, 1. I''ll count unread per page myself.\n\nActually another thought: maybe most of the \"edge\" fixtures (att-*, jsonld-*, html-*) are unread and there are ~100 of them. Plus the 200-message long thread all unread. So unread could be ~280 of ~410. Let''s just count.\n\nProceed with the batch of 5 pages."}],"createdAt":1794306228543},{"type":"tool-invocation","toolInvocation":{"state":"result","result":{"mailbox":"ana@demo.trymetis.email","cou