 to Apr 12 (index 311). All read. 50. unread 0.\n\nD) token 351 → returned Jul 22 (351) down to Jan 12 2026 (301). All read. 50.\n\nE) token 311 → returned Apr 11 (311) down to Jan 12 (301)? The second page of batch B ended at \"Priya Nair 12 Jan 2026 Type licences\" and token 301. That''s indices 311..301? No, that would be only 11 items. Hmm, it returned 50 items: from Apr 11 2026 (index 310) back to Jan 12 2026 (index 301)? That''s 10. Let me recount: the E page (token 311) list: Apr 11 310, Mar 27 309, Mar 27 308, Mar 22 307, Mar 20 306, Mar 2 305, Feb 10 304, Feb 9 303, Jan 21 302, Jan 12 301, then Jan 1 300, Dec 12 299, Nov 22 298, Nov 2 297, Oct 13 296, Sep 23 295, Sep 3 294, Aug 14 293, Jul 25 292, Jul 5 291, Jun 15 290, May 26 289, May 6 288, Apr 16 287, Mar 27 286, Mar 7 285, Feb 15 284, Jan 26 283, Jan 6 282, Dec 17 281, Nov 27 280, Nov 7 279, then Edge Sender 278 (html-base64...), 277, 276, 275, 274, 273, 272, 271, 270, 269, 268, 267, 266, 265, 264, 263, 262, 261. That''s 50 items (310→261). Yes! And next token 261. So indices 310..261.\n\nNote: among those, the Edge Sender indices 278..261 are isRead:false. Count: 278,277,276,275,274,273,272,271,270,269,268,267,266,265,264,263,262,261 = 18 unread. (278 down to 261 = 18 items.)\n\nF) token 301 → returned Jan 1 2026 (index 300?) ... wait, the page with token 301 returned: Jan 1 2026, Dec 12 2025, Nov 22, Nov 2, Oct 13, Sep 23, Sep 3, Aug 14, Jul 25, Jul 5, Jun 15, May 26, May 6, Apr 16, Mar 27, Mar 7, Feb 15, Jan 26, Jan 6 2025, Dec 17 2024, Nov 27 2024, Nov 7 2024, then Edge Sender 278..261. Hmm that''s the same as E page.\n\nUgh — pages 311 and 301 gave overlapping content (both ended with edge 278..261?). The first of that batch (token 351) returned Jul 22..Jan 12 with token 301; the second (token 311) returned Apr 11..Jan 12? Actually the second returned \"Apr 11 2026... down to Jan 12 2026 (Priya Type licences)\" and token 301. Wait no, that was the batch with 351 and 311: first result = Jul 22 (350) ... Jan 12 (301) — indices 350..301, token 301. Second result = Apr 11 (310) ... Jan 12 (301)?? and token 301. Hmm inconsistent.\n\nThis is getting messy. Let me instead count precisely from what I have, using the index numbers embedded in the ids, which are authoritative.\n\nObserved unread items (isRead:false) in the inbox:\n- Indices 278, 277, 276, 275, 274, 273, 272, 271, 270, 269, 268, 267, 266, 265, 264, 263, 262, 261 → from page E (att-*, jsonld-*, html-* ...). Let me verify from the listing: the page with token 311 (or 301) listed:\n  - 278 html-base64-inline-data-uri-15mb (unread)\n  - 277 html-css-20mb\n  - 276 html-no-visible-text\n  - 275 html-hostile\n  - 274 jsonld-50-blocks\n  - 273 jsonld-hostile-urls\n  - 272 jsonld-wrong-types\n  - 271 jsonld-deep-5000\n  - 270 jsonld-1mb\n  - 269 jsonld-invalid\n  - 268 att-mislabeled-zip-as-pdf\n  - 267 att-eml-nested-30\n  - 266 att-pkpass-zip-bomb\n  - 265 att-pkpass-invalid-json\n  - 264 att-pkpass-no-pass-json\n  - 263 att-pkpass-corrupt-zip\n  - 262 att-vcf-1000-contacts\n  - 261 att-vcf-malformed\n  → 18 unread. Yes, that listing (page with token 261... hmm). Actually that listing was the \"261\" call result? It returned 10 items (260..251): att-ics-rrule-secondly (260), att-ics-10000-events (259), att-ics-invalid (258), att-pdf-2000-pages (257), att-pdf-corrupt (256), att-pdf-empty (255), att-binary-30mb (254), att-hostile-filenames (253), att-no-filename (252), att-zero-byte (251). Wait — that was the call with pageToken 261, and it returned exactly those 10, all unread (isRead false). Then there were also 278..261 in the earlier listing (the one that had 50 items ending with \"att-zero-byte\"? no).\n\nLet me carefully reconstruct: The call with pageToken=261 returned 10 emails: indices 260..251, all Edge Sender, all unread, and NO nextPageToken? It didn''t show nextPageToken — right, that response had no nextPageToken field, meaning end of list? Hmm, but that would mean index 251 is the last... but then later calls with token 251, 201, 151, 101, 51, 1 returned more. So the 261 page did have a next token? The response showed only 10 items; I don''t recall a nextPageToken. Whatever.\n\nGiven the confusion, let me just enumerate every inbox item I have seen with its index and read status, then determine the unread count. But there''s risk of duplicates/misses.\n\nBetter approach: the inbox seems to contain indices 1..410. Real mail: indices ~279..410 (read). Edge fixtures: indices 1..278 (mostly unread).\n\nLet me count unread among the ones I actually observed:\n\nPage with token 251 → indices 250..201: all \"Re: Long thread\" Edge Sender, all isRead:false → 50 unread.\nPage token 201 → indices 200..151: all unread → 50.\nPage token 151 → indices 150..101: all unread → 50.\nPage token 101 → indices 100..51: all unread → 50.\nPage token 51 → indices 50..1: 50 items, all unread (indices 50 down to 1: 42,44,38,50,49,48,47,46,37,36,35,34,33,32,31,30,29,28,27,26,25,24,23,22,21,20,19,18,17,16,15,14,13,12,11,10,9,8,7,6,5,4,45,43,40,39,3,2,1 — that''s 50 items, all unread: yes all isRead:false).\nPage token 1 → returned 0 items (empty). Good — so index 1 was included in page 51, and the list ends there.\n\nAlso the earlier page (token 261) returned 10 items: indices 260..251, all unread.\nAnd the page(s) containing indices 278..261: which page was that? It was the page with token 311 or 301 — the one that had 50 items: from \"Apr 11 2026\" down through newsletters to... let me re-read that result (the second result in the batch where I passed 351 and 311). Its content: started Apr 11 2026 (index 310), Mar 27, Mar 27, Mar 22, Mar 20, Mar 2, Feb 10, Feb 9, Jan 21, Jan 12, Jan 1, Dec 12, Nov 22, Nov 2, Oct 13, Sep 23, Sep 3, Aug 14, Jul 25, Jul 5, Jun 15, May 26, May 6, Apr 16, Mar 27, Mar 7, Feb 15, Jan 26, Jan 6, Dec 17 2024, Nov 27 2024, Nov 7 2024, then Edge: 278 html-base64..., 277, 276, 275, 274, 273, 272, 271, 270, 269, 268, 267, 266, 265, 264, 263, 262, 261. Count: 32 + 18 = 50. Yes! So indices 310..261, token 261. That page listed index 261 (att-vcf-malformed)? Hmm the last item in that listing was \"att-vcf-malformed\" (261). And the pageToken=261 call then returned indices 260..251 (10 items) — wait, that''s inconsistent: if 261 was already returned, the next page starting at 261 would return 261 first. But it returned 260 first. Eh. Or maybe the 50-item page ended at 262 and 261 wasn''t in it. Minor.\n\nAnd the other page in that batch (token 351) returned indices 350..301 (Jul 22..Jan 12) all read.\n\nWait, but then which page covered 300..279? Hmm: page token 301 returned: Jan 12 2026 (Priya Type licences, index 301), Jan 1 2026 (300), Dec 12 (299), Nov 22 (298), Nov 2 (297), Oct 13 (296), Sep 23 (295), Sep 3 (294), Aug 14 (293), Jul 25 (292), Jul 5 (291), Jun 15 (290), May 26 (289), May 6 (288), Apr 16 (287), Mar 27 (286), Mar 7 (285), Feb 15 (284), Jan 26 (283), Jan 6 (282), Dec 17 (281), Nov 27 (280), Nov 7 (279), then Edge 278..? The page I recall (index from the batch with token 301? I called 301 and 311) — one of them returned the listing with Jan 12 2026 first and the att/jsonld items at the end. And it had 50 items total and token 261.\n\nOK. Regardless of exact pagination bookkeeping, the key numbers: edge fixtures occupy indices 1..278 roughly, and all observed edge items are unread; real mail indices 279..410 all read.\n\nTotal inbox items: the last page (token 51) covered indices 50..1 and had no nextPageToken → total = 410 items.\n\nUnread = all the edge fixtures = indices 1..278?? But careful: are ALL of 1..278 unread? I observed continuous unread runs: 1..50 (page 51), 51..100 (page 101), 101..150, 151..200, 201..250 (long thread), 251..260 (att-*), 261..278 (att/jsonld/html). That''s 1..278 = 278 items, all unread, as far as observed. Did I observe every index in 1..278? Pages: 251-page (250..201), 201-page (200..151), 151-page (150..101), 101-page (100..51), 51-page (50..1), 261-page (260..251), and the 310..261 page covering 278..261. So yes: 1..50, 51..100, 101..150, 151..200, 201..250, 251..260, 261..278 — all covered, all unread. \n\nBut wait: are indices 279..300 real mail? Index 279 Nov 7 2024 Kestrel, 280, 281, 282... 300 Jan 1 2026 — yes real mail, all read (as observed).\n\nHmm, but is index 279 Kestrel unread? Observed: \"Thu Nov 07 2024 08:00 Kestrel Supply Co. This week at Kestrel: Stoneware from Porto\" isRead: true. Yes read.\n\nSo unread in inbox = 278.\n\nHmm, that''s a big claim. Let me double check the unread flags in the real-mail region: I saw isRead: true for all real mail in Sep 2026 → Nov 2024 (indices 279-410). And for indices 1..278, all false.\n\nWait — but were there any real (non-edge) messages with isRead false in the inbox? E.g., the emails with \"isRead\":false in searchMail results were all Edge Sender or Spam ones. The inbox listing showed the unread ones are Edge Sender only. Also Konkan Bank/others all true.\n\nSo: **278 unread in the inbox**, and they are all the edge-test fixtures from \"Edge Sender\" (Jun 2024), including a single 200-message \"Re: Long thread\" thread.\n\nHmm, but let me double check total = 410. The newest real mail index is 410 (Kestrel Sep 26). The page with no token covered 50..1 and returned nothing after. So total inbox = 410. Unread 278, read 132.\n\nLet me sanity check read count: 410-278 = 132. The real mail region 279..410 = 132 items. That matches exactly: all real mail read, all fixtures unread. \n\nSo the answer: 278 unread, all of it one thing — a 200-message synthetic \"Re: Long thread\" thread plus ~78 other Edge Sender test fixtures; none of it is actual correspondence. Every real message (132 of them) is read.\n\nLet me verify the composition of the 278:\n- 200 messages: \"Re: Long thread\" (indices 51..250) = 200 items (indices 51-250 inclusive = 200).\n- att-* : indices 251..268 = 18 items (att-zero-byte 251 ... att-mislabeled-zip-as-pdf 268).\n- jsonld-*: 269..274 = 6.\n- html-*: 275..278 = 4