tes 2026-10-05.md 574 B — on all six readable.\n- Clients/ identical: Aozora Ceramics/, Kaltwasser GmbH/, Saffron & Sage/.\n- Finance/ identical: only 2025/ — one child, so Finance/ has a single year folder.\n- welcome.txt identical text (verified byte-for-byte on two), pointing to Clients/Finance/Team and \"Ask Sam for access to Finance/\".\n- Verified content equality on two drives for both welcome.txt and the meeting note.\n\nDifferences that are real:\n1. Two S3 buckets carry an extra top-level `edge/` — 12 folders + 3 files (empty.txt 0 B, no-content-type.bin 4,096 B, one-byte 1 B) — test fixtures, not studio work.\n2. The scoped FTP drive exposes only Clients/ — a permission boundary, not a difference in content. So it \"matches\" only insofar as we''re allowed to look.\n3. Metadata only: S3 returns no mimeType and no modifiedAt; WebDAV calls the note text/plain where others say text/markdown. Same bytes.\n\nSo: the seven drives are replicas of one shared drive (\"the Fernhill Studio shared drive\"). That means searches across all drives will return each hit 5-7 times; and any write will land in one endpoint only, so the copies will drift.\n\nThat last point is genuinely useful: editing in one place leaves the others stale. Also note the S3 versions are the only ones with mimeType missing — cosmetic.\n\nCareful about one nuance: drive 3 has only Clients/ visible; does it really mirror? Can''t tell beyond Clients/, and its Clients/ matches.\n\nAlso: five drives show the same four top-level entries; drive 6/7 show five. So \"same files\" = yes except the edge/ fixtures on the S3 pair.\n\nWrite the answer with a compact table + notes. Offer: identify which endpoint is the master / dedupe warning.\n\nOne more consideration: modifiedAt on SFTP/WebDAV shows 2026-11-10T10:16:04Z — i.e., today, ~10 minutes before the turn. That''s just the listing/crawl time probably; I shouldn''t over-read it. Skip.\n\nLet me write.","details":[{"type":"text","text":"Confirmed: identical content. Formatting trap fixed: pipe tables, nested list, blockquote, code fence.\n\nNow answer: Yes — they''re mirrors. Same Fernhill Studio corpus on 7 endpoints.\n\nPresent:\n- Same folder skeleton: Team/, Finance/, Clients/, welcome.txt on 5; S3s add edge/; scoped FTP shows only Clients/.\n- Team/ identical: website/, Offsite 2026/, 新しいロゴ案.png 11,491 B, voice-memo.wav 88,244 B, brand-tokens.json 240 B, Meeting notes 2026-10-05.md 574 B — on all six readable.\n- Clients/ identical: Aozora Ceramics/, Kaltwasser GmbH/, Saffron & Sage/.\n- Finance/ identical: only 2025/ — one child, so Finance/ has a single year folder.\n- welcome.txt identical text (verified byte-for-byte on two), pointing to Clients/Finance/Team and \"Ask Sam for access to Finance/\".\n- Verified content equality on two drives for both welcome.txt and the meeting note.\n\nDifferences that are real:\n1. Two S3 buckets carry an extra top-level `edge/` — 12 folders + 3 files (empty.txt 0 B, no-content-type.bin 4,096 B, one-byte 1 B) — test fixtures, not studio work.\n2. The scoped FTP drive exposes only Clients/ — a permission boundary, not a difference in content. So it \"matches\" only insofar as we''re allowed to look.\n3. Metadata only: S3 returns no mimeType and no modifiedAt; WebDAV calls the note text/plain where others say text/markdown. Same bytes.\n\nSo: the seven drives are replicas of one shared drive (\"the Fernhill Studio shared drive\"). That means searches across all drives will return each hit 5-7 times; and any write will land in one endpoint only, so the copies will drift.\n\nThat last point is genuinely useful: editing in one place leaves the others stale. Also note the S3 versions are the only ones with mimeType missing — cosmetic.\n\nCareful about one nuance: drive 3 has only Clients/ visible; does it really mirror? Can''t tell beyond Clients/, and its Clients/ matches.\n\nAlso: five drives show the same four top-level entries; drive 6/7 show five. So \"same files\" = yes except the edge/ fixtures on the S3 pair.\n\nWrite the answer with a compact table + notes. Offer: identify which endpoint is the master / dedupe warning.\n\nOne more consideration: modifiedAt on SFTP/WebDAV shows 2026-11-10T10:16:04Z — i.e., today, ~10 minutes before the turn. That''s just the listing/crawl time probably; I shouldn''t over-read it. Skip.\n\nLet me write."}],"createdAt":1794306435496},{"type":"text","text":"**Yes — they''re mirrors of the same drive.** All seven hold one corpus, *\"the Fernhill Studio shared drive\"*, and where I could read both sides the bytes match exactly.\n\n**Same skeleton (5 drives):** `Clients/`, `Finance/`, `Team/`, `welcome.txt` — in every FTP, SFTP, WebDAV and S3 drive.\n\n**Same contents underneath:**\n| Path | Entries | Where verified |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Team/` | `website/`, `Offsite 2026/`, `新しいロゴ案.png` (11,491 B), `voice-memo.wav` (88,244 B), `brand-tokens.json` (240 B), `Meeting notes 2026-10-05.md` (574 B) | identical names **and sizes** on all 6 readable drives |\n| `Clients/` | `Aozora Ceramics/`, `Kaltwasser GmbH/`, `Saffron & Sage/` | identical on SFTP drive 1 and S3 drive 6 |\n| `Finance/` | `2025/` — one folder, nothing else | identical on drives 1 and 6 |\n| `welcome.txt` | 182 B | read on drive 1 and drive 6 — **character-for-character identical**: client work in `Clients/`, invoices in `Finance/`, rest in `Team/`, \"Ask Sam for access to Finance/\" |\n\nI also pulled `Team/Meeting notes 2026-10-05.md` from the SFTP drive and from the second S3 bucket — the same note, the same bytes, down to the budget table and the print-size code block.\n\n**Three differences, none of them content:**\n\n- **Drives 6 and 7 (both S3) carry an extra top-level `edge/`** — 12 folders (`big`, `bulk-2500`, `deep`, `docs`, `mislabeled`, `unicode`, `empty-folder`…) and 3 files: `empty.txt` 0 B, `no-content-type.bin` 4,096 B, `one-byte` 1 B. Test fixtures. The FTP, SFTP and WebDAV drives don''t have it.\n- **Drive 3 (ftp, the scoped one) shows only `Clients/`.** That''s your permission grant, not a missing folder — its `Clients/` matches the others, and nothing above it is visible to us.\n- **Metadata only:** the S3 buckets report no `mimeType` and no modified time; WebDAV labels the meeting note `text/plain` where the others say `text/markdown`. Same file.\n\nTwo practical consequences:\n\n1. **Every global search will hand you the same hit 5–7 times.** Searching \"brand tokens\" or \"Aozora\" will return one S3 copy, one WebDAV copy, three FTP copies and an SFTP copy — same file, different `driveId`. I''ll name the drive whenever I report a result so you know which copy you''re looking at.\n2. **They only stay identical if nothing writes through one endpoint.** A file created or renamed via the FTP drive lands there alone — the other six go stale. If you want a canonical copy, I can tell you which endpoint your newest files land on first; otherwise treat one of them as the master and leave the rest read-only in your head.","createdAt":1794306435496}],"metadata":{"modelId":"deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash:floor","provider":"openai-compatible.chat"},"content":"**Yes — they''re mirrors of the same drive.** All seven hold one corpus, *\"the Fernhill Studio shared drive\"*, and where I could read both sides the bytes match exactly.\n\n**Same skeleton (5 drives):** `Clients/`, `Finance/`, `Team/`, `welcome.txt` — in every FTP, SFTP, WebDAV and S3 drive.\n\n**Same contents underneath:**\n| Path | Entries | Where verified |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Team/` | `website/`, `Offsite 2026/`, `新しいロゴ案.png` (11,491 B), `voice-memo.wav` (88,244 B), `brand-tokens.json` (240 B), `Meeting notes 2026-10-05.md` (574 B) | identical names **and sizes** on all 6 readable drives |\n| `Clients/` | `Aozora Ceramics/`, `Kaltwasser GmbH/`, `Saffron & Sage/` | identical on SFTP drive 1 and S3 drive 6 |\n| `Finance/` | `2025/` — one folder, nothing else | identical on drives 1 and 6 |\n| `welcome.txt` | 182 B | read on drive 1 and drive 6 — **character-for-character identical**: client work in `Clients/`, invoices in `Finance/`, rest in `Team/`, \"Ask Sam for access to Finance/\" |\n\nI also pulled `Team/Meeting notes 2026-10-05.md` from the SFTP drive and from the second S3 bucket — the same note, the same bytes, down to the budget table and the print-size code block.\n\n**Three differences, none of them content:**\n\n- **Drives 6 and 7 (both S3) carry an extra top-level `edge/`** — 12 folders (`big`, `bulk-2500`, `deep`, `docs`, `mislabeled`, `unicode`, `empty-folder`…) and 3 files: `empty.txt` 0 B, `no-content-type.bin` 4,096 B, `one-byte` 1 B. Test fixtures. The FTP, SFTP and WebDAV drives don''t have it.\n- **Drive 3 (ftp, the scoped one) shows only `Clients/`.** That''s your permission grant, not a missing folder — its `Clients/` matches the others, and nothing above it is visible to us.\n- **Metadata only:** the S3 buckets report no `mimeType` and no modified time; WebDAV labels the meeting note `text/plain` where the others say `text/markdown`. Same file.\n\nTwo practical consequences:\n\n1. **Every global search will hand you the same hit 5–7 times.** Searching \"brand tokens\" or \"Aozora\" will return one S3 copy, one WebDAV copy, three FTP copies and an SFTP copy — same file, different `driveId`. I''ll name the drive whenever I report a result so you know which copy you''re looking at.\n2. **They only stay identical if nothing writes through one endpoint.** A file created or renamed via the FTP drive lands there alone — the other six go stale. If you want a canonical copy, I can tell you which endpoint your newest files land on first; otherwise treat one of them as the master and leave the rest read-only in your head."}','assistant','v2','2026-11-10T10:26:16.040Z','f0c4be98-b2fd-59d7-b11e-c921e7d22615');
